Approach What you add to <code>mkdocs.yml</code> How you write a diagram in Markdown Pros Cons 1 · Client-side compile with TikZJax <code>yaml\nextra_css:\n  - https://tikzjax.com/v1/fonts.css\nextra_javascript:\n  - https://tikzjax.com/v1/tikzjax.js\n</code> <code>markdown\n&lt;script type=\"text/tikz\"&gt;\n\\usepackage{circuitikz}\n\\begin{circuitikz}\n\\draw (0,0) to[R,l=$1\\,\\mathrm{k}\\Omega$] (3,0) to[C,l=$10\\,\\mu\\text{F}$] (6,0)\n      (6,0) -- (6,-2) -- (0,-2) -- (0,0);\n\\end{circuitikz}\n&lt;/script&gt;\n</code> Nothing to install; live preview still instant with <code>mkdocs serve</code>; diagrams stay as clean SVG First render happens in the reader’s browser → page load gets slower if you embed dozens of large schematics; needs JS enabled; relies on external CDN (can self-host) (tikzjax.com, GitHub) 2 · Pre-compile to SVG during the MkDocs build <code>yaml\nplugins:\n  - search\n  - tikzfig  # custom plugin shown below\n</code> <code>markdown\n</code>circuitikz\n\draw (0,0) to[sV,l=$V_s$] (0,3) -- (3,3)\n            to[R,l=$R$]      (3,0) -- (0,0);\n``` Pages load instantly (SVG already generated); works with JS disabled; assets are cached, so rebuilds after edits are fast You install a TeX engine (<code>tectonic</code>) + <code>dvisvgm</code>; need to drop the tiny plugin file into your repo Minimal custom plugin (<code>plugins/tikzfig.py</code>) for option 2 <pre><code># pip install tectonic
# pip install mkdocs
import hashlib, os, re, subprocess, tempfile, textwrap
from mkdocs.plugins import BasePlugin

class TikzFigPlugin(BasePlugin):
    BLOCK = re.compile(r'```circuitikz(.*?)```', re.S)

    def on_page_markdown(self, md, page, cfg, files):
        def _repl(m):
            body = m.group(1).strip()
            digest = hashlib.md5(body.encode()).hexdigest()
            rel_svg = f'assets/tikz/{digest}.svg'
            abs_svg = os.path.join(cfg["site_dir"], rel_svg)
            if not os.path.exists(abs_svg):
                tex = textwrap.dedent(f"""
                \\documentclass[border=1pt]{{standalone}}
                \\usepackage{{circuitikz}}
                \\begin{document}
                \\begin{{circuitikz}}[american]
                {body}
                \\end{{circuitikz}}
                \\end{document}
                """)
                with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as d:
                    tex_file = os.path.join(d, "fig.tex")
                    open(tex_file, "w").write(tex)
                    subprocess.run(["tectonic", "-Z", "shell-escape", tex_file], cwd=d, check=True)
                    subprocess.run(["dvisvgm", "--no-fonts", "fig.dvi", "-o", abs_svg], cwd=d, check=True)
            return f'![]({{ static }}{rel_svg})'
        return self.BLOCK.sub(_repl, md)
</code></pre>

Prompt history <p>Version 2.0.0 (2026-10-14). Ported from p5.js 1.10.0 to p5.js 2.3.2 and made responsive (one <code>createCanvas</code> call, <code>updateCanvasSize()</code>, <code>windowResized()</code>); the legacy entry file <code>nyquist-shannon-sampling.html</code> now redirects to <code>main.html</code>. Samples are now taken at the exact times \(t = n/f_s\). The earlier sketch stepped through the canvas in whole pixels (<code>floor(width / samplingRate)</code>), so the true number of samples per period differed from the label (4.5 instead of the 4.4 shown at the default setting on a 450 px canvas) and changed with the canvas width. The slider keeps its range, step and default (1 to 50, 0.1, 22); its value is now labeled as the sampling rate in Hz for the 5 Hz signal shown over one second, with samples per period (\(f_s/5\), the old label) in the read-out. The lower plot keeps the original straight-line reconstruction (now gray) and adds the ideal sinc reconstruction (blue), a legend, a time axis and the Nyquist status line with the alias frequency.</p> <p>Original prompt (sent to ChatGPT o1-preview)</p> <pre><code>Please help me create a p5.js simulation that helps
students understand Sampling and Aliasing. The Nyquist-Shannon
sampling theorem and the concept of aliasing involve understanding
how continuous signals are represented in discrete form. Grasping
how undersampling leads to frequency overlap (aliasing) can be non-intuitive.

I would like an Interactive p5.js program that can demonstrate how
varying the sampling rate affects the reconstructed signal.
By adjusting parameters in real-time, students can visualize
aliasing effects and the importance of adhering to the Nyquist rate.

Please create a p5.js simulation that allows students to use a
slider to adjust the sampling rate for a waveform using a slider.
Display two waveforms. One is the input with the samples drawn
as radius 7 red circles. The second waveform is one done from
the reconstruction of the sampling points.
Place the original on the top and the reconstruction under
the original.

Use the following template:
</code></pre> <p>Template supplied with the prompt</p> <pre><code>// region drawing parameters
